<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matthäus Alber</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1495–1570</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matthäus Alber was a Lutheran reformer known as the Luther of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. As preacher in Reutlingen from 1521 he led the city into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and in 1530 Reutlingen was one of the two cities, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>. Refusing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> Interim in 1548, he later served the Duchy of Württemberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and as abbot of Blaubeuren.</p></div>
